[Fixed-point restriction and normal weights] [Atiyah–Bott, Section 3, printed pp. 7–9](https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~mmovshev/MAT570Spring2008/BOOKS/atiyahbott_moment.pdf)

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The circle action on $\mathbb{CP}^1$, the coefficient field $k$, and the product Milnor Borel model.

[F1] The character localization definition gives $A_T(k)=k[u]$, with $u$ the degree-two class of the weight-one character, and $-u$ is the class of the inverse character. The current real Borel supplier has a fresh-readiness-record gap; use of its real interface is conditional. [[def-qftx-character-localization]]

[F2] The fixed-point and normal-weight conventions are those of the compact torus fixed-locus lemma. [[lem-qftx-compact-torus-fixed-locus-normal-weights]]

[F3] Restriction to the fixed set becomes an isomorphism after inverting the nontrivial character classes. [[thm-borel-localization-for-compact-torus-actions]]

## Verification

**Given:** The action and conventions in the Example.

1.1 A point $[z_0:z_1]$ is fixed by all $t\in T$ only if one homogeneous coordinate is zero: if both were nonzero, equality $[z_0:e^{2\pi it}z_1]=[z_0:z_1]$ would force $e^{2\pi it}=1$ for every $t$. Thus the fixed points are $p_+$ and $p_-$. In the local coordinate $w=z_1/z_0$ at $p_+$, the action is $w\mapsto e^{2\pi it}w$. In $v=z_0/z_1$ at $p_-$, it is $v\mapsto e^{-2\pi it}v$. These give the stated weights and character classes $u$ and $-u$, both inverted by $\mathscr S_T(k)$. [F1, F2, algebra]

2.1 Since $F$ is a pair of fixed points, $ET\times_TF=(BT\times\{p_+\})\amalg(BT\times\{p_-\})$, so $H_T^*(F;k)=A_T(k)\oplus A_T(k)$. Localizing this finite sum gives the displayed target, and [F3] identifies the restriction map with the asserted isomorphism. [F1, F3, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
